<Editorial
  title="The evidence behind the advice."
  description="How we research products, build a shortlist, and keep the limits of our evidence visible."
>
  <p>
    Our goal is to make a printer decision understandable. We separate manufacturer specifications,
    editorial reasoning, and user-entered estimates so you can see what a recommendation rests on.
  </p>
  <h2>What we verify</h2>
  <p>
    We consult official product pages, user manuals, driver tables, and support documentation. Each
    catalog entry records the exact model variant, documented connections and devices, media format,
    key specifications, source URLs, and a last-checked date.
  </p>
  <p>
    The current catalog was checked on {catalogCheckedLabel}. Price references have their own dates.
    Documentation can change. Driver availability and supported OS releases must be checked again
    before purchase. Manufacturer-rated speeds are not independently measured results.
  </p>
  <h2>How the shortlist is selected</h2>
  <p>
    The initial three printers represent different workflows: a Wi-Fi model that spans devices, a
    dedicated USB desktop option, and a 300 dpi networked model with a cutter. This is a
    deliberately small, research-based selection. It is not an exhaustive market ranking.
  </p>
  <p>
    We prioritize a documented 4-inch shipping-label workflow, clear model identification, a
    meaningful connection or media distinction, and accessible manufacturer evidence. We do not call
    one printer universally best.
  </p>
  <h2>How the Finder works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Platform:</strong> changes setup guidance. We do not assume native marketplace
      integrations or award points for a platform name.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Operating system:</strong> excludes models without a documented workflow for that
      device category. Version and app restrictions remain visible.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Order volume:</strong> changes advice about media and batch handling. It is not
      converted into an unsupported durability claim.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Connection:</strong> excludes printers without the requested print connection.
      Bluetooth-assisted setup is not treated as Bluetooth printing. Mobile USB and insufficiently
      verified Mac/mobile Bluetooth combinations are excluded.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Budget:</strong> excludes a model when its dated verified hardware price is above the
      cap. An unknown price is explicitly flagged and cannot be guaranteed to fit.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Among eligible options, an explicitly requested USB setup favors fewer connection extras. At 250
    or more weekly orders, generic-media models are listed first to make batch-stock handling easier
    to investigate. These preferences use documented product features, not brand names or arbitrary
    quality scores. Otherwise, tied matches retain catalog order; being listed first does not imply
    better print quality. Budget is an eligibility check, not an upsell rule. Affiliate data is
    never an input.
  </p>
  <p>If no model meets the verified criteria, the Finder says so and explains the exclusions.</p>
  <h2>What we have not tested</h2>
  <p>
    We have not measured print quality, barcode-scan success, installation time, long-term
    reliability, service response, or daily duty cycles. We publish no star ratings or “tested by
    us” claims. Future hands-on work must describe its sample, conditions, date, and limits
    separately.
  </p>
  <h2>How costs are estimated</h2>
  <p>
    The calculator uses one label per order, 52 weeks per year, a one-time printer purchase, your
    waste percentage, and your media price. Waste is modeled as a proportion of labels consumed. The
    required label count is rounded up.
  </p>
  <p>
    Prices entered into the tool are assumptions, not live offers. Consumption is valued
    proportionally; buying whole packs can require a larger cash outlay. Postage, tax, electricity,
    repairs, financing, subscriptions, and labor are excluded.
  </p>
  <h2>Images and commercial relationships</h2>
  <p>
    Real products use manufacturer photographs and are credited accordingly. Original AI-generated
    imagery is used only for generic editorial scenes and is never presented as a photograph of a
    real printer model.
  </p>
  <p>
    Affiliate links, when configured, are disclosed near the link and marked as sponsored.
    Commercial relationships do not alter the filtering or ranking rules. See our{' '}
    <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a>.
  </p>
  <h2>Keeping the evidence current</h2>
  <p>
    A check date describes when a source was consulted; it is not a promise that the information
    remains unchanged. When source documents conflict, we prefer the exact model's technical table
    over general family-level marketing, and retain a caution where the conflict is unresolved.
  </p>
  <p>
    Follow the manufacturer evidence on each product page before committing to a purchase,
    especially for recently released OS versions and similarly named hardware variants.
  </p>
  <p>
    Send factual corrections and supporting public sources to{' '}
    <a href="mailto:hello.jclabs@gmail.com">hello.jclabs@gmail.com</a>. See{' '}
    <a href="/contact/">contact and corrections</a> for the details that help us review a claim.
  </p>
</Editorial>
